<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<title>{{ title }}</title>
<style>{{ css }}</style>
</head>
<body>

{# ------------------------------------------------------------------ cover #}
<section class="cover">
  <div>
    <img class="cover-mark" src="{{ mark }}" alt="">
  </div>
  <div>
    <div class="rule"></div>
    <div class="tagline">Distributed Teaching Collaboratives Consortium</div>
    <h1>{{ title }}</h1>
    <p class="blurb">{{ blurb }}</p>
  </div>
  <div class="meta">
    <strong>{{ count }} contributions</strong> &nbsp;·&nbsp; {{ institutions|length }} institutions
    &nbsp;·&nbsp; {{ cohort }}<br>
    Generated {{ generated }} &nbsp;·&nbsp; {{ repo_url }}
  </div>
</section>

{# ------------------------------------------------------------------ front matter #}
<section class="fm">
  <h1>About this handbook</h1>

  <p>This handbook collects every teaching contribution published in the DTCC-MLU repository, in
  full, so that it can be read and taught from without going online. It is generated directly from
  that repository, so what you are reading is the same material the repository holds &mdash; not a
  summary of it.</p>

  <p>Each part covers one contribution: what it is, who it is for, what a participant walks away
  with, and then the complete teaching materials &mdash; assignments, rubrics, facilitator guides,
  walkthroughs and handouts. Where a contribution ships slide decks or readings as their own
  documents, those are reproduced as appendices at the end of that part.</p>

  <h2>What cannot be delivered on paper</h2>

  <p>Some material in these contributions is only usable in its original form, and this handbook is
  honest about the boundary rather than letting you discover it halfway through a session:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Jupyter notebooks</strong> are printed here so you can read the instructional
      narrative and see the code, but they have to be <em>run</em> from the repository. Four
      contributions are built around one.</li>
    <li><strong>Hosted applications</strong> &mdash; the PartyRock and Amazon Quick apps &mdash; are
      live objects on someone else's servers. They can only be linked to, and their owners can
      change or withdraw them.</li>
    <li><strong>Video modules</strong> are hosted externally and are linked by URL.</li>
    <li><strong>Sample data files</strong> that a notebook loads &mdash; source documents, rosters,
      transcripts &mdash; are referenced rather than reprinted, since they are inputs to a program
      rather than something to read.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Every such URL in this handbook is written out in full, so it can be typed from a printed
  page.</p>

  <h2>Getting the digital materials</h2>

  <p>Everything here, plus the notebooks, sample data and source files, is at:</p>

  <p style="font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 11pt;">{{ repo_url }}</p>

  <p>You do not need an account to browse or download. Each contribution directory contains a
  <code>README.md</code> that opens it, a <code>materials/</code> folder holding the deliverables,
  and a <code>mlu-contribution.yml</code> carrying its structured metadata. If a contribution has a
  published release, its download link appears in that contribution's overview page here.</p>

  <p>To request materials, report a problem, or submit your own contribution, open an issue in that
  repository, or contact the maintainers listed on each contribution's overview page.</p>

  <h2>Reuse</h2>

  <p>Contributions are licensed individually &mdash; there is no single licence covering this
  handbook. Documentation is generally <code>CC-BY-4.0</code> and code <code>MIT</code>, but bundled
  data keeps its own per-file terms and a few items are linked rather than licensed for
  redistribution. The licensing section at the back records the terms for every contribution, and
  any exception is stated in that contribution's own pages. Check before you redistribute.</p>
</section>

{% if sequences %}
<section class="fm">
  <h1>Suggested sequences</h1>
  <p>Some of these contributions are designed to be taught in a particular order. These groupings
  are drawn from the curated collections in the repository.</p>
  {% for seq in sequences %}
  <h2>{{ seq.title }}</h2>
  <p>{{ seq.summary }}</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th style="width:12mm">#</th><th style="width:60mm">Contribution</th><th>Why it sits here</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for m in seq.members %}
      <tr>
        <td>{{ m.order }}</td>
        <td><a href="#{{ m.id }}">{{ m.title }}</a><br><span class="muted small" style="font-family:var(--mono)">{{ m.id }}</span></td>
        <td>{{ m.note }}</td>
      </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  {% endfor %}
</section>
{% endif %}

{# ------------------------------------------------------------------ contents #}
<section class="fm toc">
  <h1>Contents</h1>
  {{ toc_html }}
</section>

{# ------------------------------------------------------------------ the parts #}
{{ parts_html }}

{# ------------------------------------------------------------------ back matter #}
<section class="fm">
  <h1>Licensing and credits</h1>

  <p>Every contribution in this handbook is licensed by its own authors on its own terms. Nothing
  here grants rights beyond what each contributor granted. Where bundled data carries a separate
  licence, or where material is linked rather than redistributed, that is recorded below and in the
  contribution's own pages.</p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th style="width:24mm">ID</th>
        <th>Contribution and authors</th>
        <th style="width:26mm">Documentation</th>
        <th style="width:20mm">Code</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for d in docs %}
      <tr>
        <td style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:8pt">{{ d.id }}</td>
        <td><strong>{{ d.title }}</strong><br><span class="small muted">{{ d.byline }}</span></td>
        <td>{{ d.content_license }}</td>
        <td>{{ d.software_license or "—" }}</td>
      </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  {% if data_credits %}
  <h2>Bundled data and sources</h2>
  <p>Sample data shipped with these contributions, and where it came from. Anything not listed here
  is original material by the contribution's authors.</p>
  <table class="wide">
    <thead><tr><th style="width:22mm">ID</th><th style="width:52mm">File</th><th style="width:24mm">Licence</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for c in data_credits %}
      <tr>
        <td style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:7.5pt">{{ c.id }}</td>
        <td style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:7.5pt">{{ c.path }}</td>
        <td>{{ c.license }}</td>
        <td>{{ c.source }}</td>
      </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  {% endif %}

  <h2>About DTCC</h2>
  <p>The Distributed Teaching Collaboratives Consortium is a national, faculty-led network
  co-creating open-source curricula and student pathways in AI and robotics. These contributions
  were developed through the AWS Machine Learning University Faculty Fellows programme.</p>
  <p style="font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 10pt;">https://dtcconsortium.org</p>

  <p class="small muted" style="margin-top:8mm">This handbook was generated from the DTCC-MLU
  repository on {{ generated }} by <code>scripts/build_handbook.py</code>. To regenerate it, or to
  check whether a newer edition exists, see {{ repo_url }}.</p>
</section>

</body>
</html>
